  <h1>What Is Colloidal Gold?</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Colloidal gold is a suspension of gold nanoparticles — typically around 40 nm — that serves as the visible reporter in lateral-flow rapid tests. Antibodies are adsorbed onto the gold surface; when the labeled complex accumulates at a capture line, the concentrated particles appear as a red line readable by eye.</p>

  <h2>What colloidal gold is</h2>
  <p>Colloidal gold consists of nanometre-scale metallic gold particles suspended in liquid. Their small size and surface chemistry make them an ideal label: they are dense enough to produce an intense color when concentrated, yet stable enough to conjugate to proteins.</p>

  <h2>Why gold</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Visible</strong> — gold nanoparticles appear red at test concentrations, readable without an instrument.</li>
    <li><strong>Stable</strong> — gold is chemically inert and stable at room temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Conjugatable</strong> — antibodies adsorb onto the gold surface by electrostatic and hydrophobic interaction.</li>
    <li><strong>High signal density</strong> — a small captured amount produces a clearly visible line.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it produces the result</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Antibodies are conjugated to gold nanoparticles (see <a href="/knowledge/technology/conjugation/">colloidal gold conjugation</a>).</li>
    <li>The gold-labeled antibody binds the target analyte in the sample.</li>
    <li>The labeled complex is captured at the test line by immobilized antibodies.</li>
    <li>Accumulated gold forms a visible red line, whose intensity reflects the amount of captured label.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Particle size and performance</h2>
  <p>Particle size influences signal and flow. Larger particles give stronger color but may flow less freely and bind less efficiently; smaller particles flow better but give weaker signal. The optimal size (~20–40 nm) balances signal intensity, conjugate stability and membrane flow — a formulation choice, not a fixed universal value.</p>

  <h2>Advantages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Instrument-free visual detection</li>
    <li>Room-temperature-stable label</li>
    <li>Low-cost, scalable manufacturing</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Visual readout is qualitative (faint lines are subjective)</li>
    <li>Conjugation quality and particle aggregation affect reproducibility</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>Why is the line red?</strong> Gold nanoparticles appear red when concentrated at the capture line.</p>
    <p><strong>What size are colloidal gold particles?</strong> Typically around 20–40 nm for lateral-flow tests, chosen to balance signal and flow.</p>
    <p><strong>Is colloidal gold the only label?</strong> No — colored latex and fluorescent particles are also used; fluorescent labels enable reader-based quantification.</p>
  </div>

